Edward Snowden has proved himself to be  one of the or if not the most  influential whistleblower of our  generation hi I'm Nick Gillespie with  reason TV and today we're talking with  Trevor Timm he is the executive director  of the freedom of the press foundation  which is one of the groups that is  pushing for a pardon for Edward Snowden  right as the new movie by Oliver Stone  about edward sun comes out so Trevor  thanks for talking to us thanks for  having me you're pushing for a pardon  for Edward Snowden why are you doing  that and why now well you know I think  that Edward Snowden has proved himself  to be one of the or if not the most  influential whistleblower of our  generation you know we look back at the  three years since he came forward the  amount of change that has happened with  the US surveillance system the fact that  courts ruled it unconstitutional  directly related to his disclosures the  fact that Congress passed first  intelligence reform since the 1970s he  has had an enormous impact on how  Americans why would the president that  he you know kind of unmask why would he  ever pardon him well you know I think  that President Obama right now is he's  winding down his presidency is probably  looking at his legacy and one of the  most disappointing aspects of his  presidency has been his treatment of  whistleblowers and the fact that they've  prosecuted more leakers in history than  any other administration and you know he  himself has said that this debate that  Snowden sparked has made the country  stronger and you know it's certainly an  outside chance that he's going to get  pardon but I think it's something that  did Obama can and will seriously  consider what do you say to people who  say look Snowden's a traitor you know  what how could you pardon him well far  from being a traitor I think that he has  shown himself to be a whistleblower and  a patriot you know enshrined in our  Constitution is the fourth amendment  that you know makes illegal searches and  seizures that don't have probable cause  law enforcement didn't go to a judge to  get a warrant and with the mass  surveillance program that Snowden  revealed where you know the US was  collecting every single phone record of  every single American  whether they were guilty or innocent you  know that's just not how we operate in  this country and Snowden really exposed  that the government was was the one that  was violating the Constitution and and I  think we should be thankful for it do  you think have his revelations and the  changes in programs that have been made  have they weaken national security and  if they have is that the price we pay  for actually not just living or  believing in a free society but actually  insisting on a free society well I think  there's two aspects to that question one  you know the NSA had to come out and  basically admit that their mass  surveillance program there from their  phone collection program with every  single phone call in their database of  every single American never stopped any  terrorist attacks beyond that I think we  do recognize that we don't give the  police or the federal government full  authority to barge into our house  whenever they want or to read our email  and that if they were to do that yes  they might solve more crimes but we lose  a lot of our freedom and the rights as  Americans and so you know when edward  stone is sticking up for these rights  it's not just about you know do we stop  an attack or stop a crime from being  committed it's about you know how we  treat each other as Americans and how we  want to live in dignity does the Snowden  movie help your case in terms of  treating Edward Snowden as a patriot and  do you think it will help in terms of  generating interest in a part yeah I  think the movie is definitely going to  help with a lot of people who may not  have heard of the story before you know  that i'm partial to citizen for the  documentary that laura poitras made the  won the academy award that actually  followed the real-life story you know  obviously this film is a dramatization  but it tells a powerful story about  number one that the NSA was operating  this surveillance system in complete  secrecy and outside the law but number  two that the person that exposed it  edward snowden really had a good  intentions behind what he was doing he  would carefully thought about it and he  ultimately did the right thing by coming  forward to journalists do you think that  media portrayal of Snowden have  changed over time and are they getting  more fair less fair more bizarre yeah I  think once now that Snowden has kind of  been in the public consciousness for  three three and a half years now the  people have gotten to know him and a lot  of people who may have initially been  suspicious are realizing that you know  he had the best intentions when he did  this that this is he revealed something  that was very important that a lot of  people care about or maybe didn't even  realize that they cared about at the  time but now have come to the  realization that the privacy online is  very important and so I you know I've  been heartened to see the reaction so  far to the pardon campaigns been only  been going for a day but the fact that  there has been such public strong public  support i think is a good sign both  Hillary Clinton and Donald Trump the  likely one of them is going to be the  next president Gary Johnson the  Libertarian candidate has said that he  considers Snowden a hero he would pardon  him leaving that aside who is the best  president to be elected for Edward  Snowden and more broadly more  importantly for questions about state  surveillance and transparency you know I  think that's a hard question to answer  because the candidates are all over the  place when it comes to surveillance you  know Donald Trump has talked about how  Snowden as a traitor and Hillary Clinton  has criticized them in the past but then  they both in other cases talked about  how there shouldn't be surveillance of  Americans going on without suspicion so  you know it's I don't know if it's  necessarily a 1 choice or the other when  we're talking specifically about Snowden  I think a pardon from from either of  them is quite a long shot what about  transparency is as a broader issue  because that's the one thing and even  Obama you know it seems the one thing  governments won't say is you know what  now that we live in a new world we're  actually going to behave differently  because we're going to get caught you  know would do either of these candidates  seem particularly good on the issue of  transparency well Donald Trump in  particular has been unbelievably awful  when it comes to press freedom for  example you know kicking reporters out  of their rallies telling me that he  wants to sue them but I don't think  either of the candidates  necessarily been beacons of transparency  you know the Clinton campaign to it has  been a lot of their scandals have been  caused by the fact that they stonewalled  and avoided the Freedom of Information  Act for four or five years and they may  not be in those position the position  there are now if they just complied with  transparency so do you think I mean in  the end maybe this is a way of talking  about it that it's we can never rely on  an individual person in government  especially the president certainly be  transparent or not and it's really going  to come from the demands of the governed  oh I think that's absolutely the  ultimate lesson that we've learned from  the Obama administration I mean you know  back when he was campaigning a lot of  transparency advocates were overjoyed  because he kept talking about this issue  a lot that he was going to be the most  transparent administration in history  and a lot of those issues he's done a  complete 180 and there's been a lot of  disappointed and dejected people in the  transparency community because of that  and so that's why it's it's extra  amazing to see people like edward snow  snowden coming forward you know kind of  forcing transparency where the  government kind of let down on their  promises well we will leave it there we  have been talking with Trevor Timm he is  the executive director of the freedom of  the press foundation part of a wide  alliance of people pushing for a pardon  for Edward Snowden Trevor thanks for  talking to reason thanks a lot
 

